title: b4.1 Emotion, slips, safety and system checks
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Ten measures. System behaviour: M-01. The walk: M-02 (how often, with whom). The person on the walk, rated live by a listener-observer: M-03 emotion, M-04 slips and recovery, M-05 conversation, M-06 safety while talking. The walkers' own view: M-07. Over weeks: M-08 loneliness. Values at risk: M-09 privacy, tone and trust codes; M-10 sensitive topics, errors and disclosure. Nothing is recorded: every observer measure is coded live (EM-02).

= Measure - System performance [M-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-01
|Name|System performance
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke. Staged-event protocol, as used for prompting systems (Mihailidis et al., 2008).
|Construct measured|Whether the System does what F1 to F6 specify, on time, and nothing more.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|(((
Staged events against a timestamped reference log (EM-01): pauses, slips, repeated stories, starters with and without consent, blocked items, sensitive questions, stops, stumbles, gate, pull-cord, drops.

Checks on a full test day: network capture at the router; file-system search of all devices for audio.

Walker model (Build B, pilot walks): the model's state labels against the listener's slip codes (M-04).
)))
|Scoring or coding scheme|Per event: correct (yes/no); latency (s). False triggers per hour. Disclosure trials: items spoken without consent or against the map (target 0). Packets leaving the home network (target 0). Audio files (target 0). Model: agreement with listener codes (κ).
|Validity and reliability|Bespoke. Depends on the accuracy of the reference log. Role-players do not slip like residents.
|Serves|Verification
|Burden|No residents (staged events). Pilot walks: as EM-02.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-PR1, UC01-F2-PR2, UC01-F3-PR3, UC02-F4-PR4, UC01-F5-PR5, UC01-F6-PR6; used-by → EM-01; operationalizes → HV-03, HV-04.

= Measure - Walks and company [M-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-02
|Name|Walks and company
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke count. Walking volume is a standard outcome in exercise studies (Forbes et al., 2015).
|Construct measured|How often and how long the resident walks, and with whom.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|Session record (F6), every walk. Baseline: the same record in A phases (EM-03), plus the care record.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Per week: walks; minutes; walks alone or with a partner; walks ended early.
|Validity and reliability|Counts. Record checked against the observer on observed walks.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|None for residents.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-CL5; used-by → EM-03; operationalizes → HV-06.

= Measure - Observed emotion [M-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-03
|Name|Observed emotion
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Observed Emotion Rating Scale (OERS; Lawton, Van Haitsma & Klapper, 1996), built for nursing-home residents with Alzheimer's disease. Validated instrument.
|Construct measured|Emotional state and emotional quality of the walk, per walker.
|Type|Quantitative (observational)
|Instrument or procedure|(((
OERS, rated live by the listener-observer (EM-02). No video, no audio stored.

Windows per walk: at the door (5 min before); on the walk (each 10 min); after (10 min, in the lounge).

Each walker rated separately.
)))
|Scoring or coding scheme|(((
Five affects: pleasure, alertness, anxiety, sadness, anger. Each rated by duration within the window: 1 never; 2 under 16 s; 3 16-59 s; 4 1-5 min; 5 over 5 min.

Per walk: positive score = mean of pleasure and alertness over the walk windows; negative score = mean of anxiety, sadness and anger. The door and after windows are reported separately: mood before, and carry-over after.
)))
|Validity and reliability|(((
Psychometrics in Lawton et al. (1996). Less reliable when rated by care staff than by trained researchers (Lawton et al., 1999): researchers only.

A second observer on 20% of walks, rating independently; ICC(2,1) ≥ .60. Training: EM-02.
)))
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Human subjects; an observer walks behind.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-CL5, UC01-F2-CL1 (no lower); used-by → EM-01 (Build A), EM-02, EM-03; operationalizes → HV-05.

= Measure - Slips and recovery [M-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-04
|Name|Slips and recovery
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke. Categories from conversation analysis (Schegloff et al., 1977) and repair in Alzheimer's disease (Orange et al., 1996).
|Construct measured|How often conversation goes off track, who repairs it, how fast, and at what cost to face.
|Type|Quantitative (observational event coding)
|Instrument or procedure|Live event coding by the listener-observer, who hears the walk through a headset (EM-02). One tablet row per slip. Codes only: no words, no content.
|Scoring or coding scheme|(((
Per slip:

* Type: T thread lost; W word not found; R story repeated; B belief from another time; N person not recognised; S sensitive question
* Repaired by: self; partner; System; staff; nobody
* Step used (IDP-01): wait; place; thread; shared topic; feeling; deferral
* Seconds from slip to repair
* Outcome: resumed; new topic; ended
* Face signs within 30 s: apology; self-criticism ("my head"); withdrawal; laughing it off

Recovered: talk resumes, on the topic or a new one, within 30 s, without face signs or distress.

Per walk: recovery rate (recovered / slips); self-repair share; median seconds to repair; face signs per slip. R slips exposed by the partner ("You told me already").
)))
|Validity and reliability|Bespoke. Nothing is recorded, so nothing can be re-coded: a second listener codes 20% of walks, live and independently; κ ≥ .60 for type and repairer (Landis & Koch, 1977). Disagreements are discussed after the walk and logged, never merged into one code.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Human subjects; a listener hears the conversation (EM-02 rules).

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F2-CL1, UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F2-CL3; used-by → EM-01 (Build A), EM-02, EM-03; operationalizes → HV-02, HV-06.

= Measure - Conversation [M-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-05
|Name|Conversation
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Interval coding adapted from the Quality of Interactions Schedule (QUIS; Dean, Proudfoot & Lindesay, 1993), developed for staff-resident interaction. The adaptation to talk between residents is bespoke.
|Construct measured|How much the walkers talk, and how well.
|Type|Quantitative (observational)
|Instrument or procedure|Listener-observer, 2-minute intervals (EM-02).
|Scoring or coding scheme|Per interval: talk between walkers (yes/no); quality: positive (warm, laughter, a shared topic), neutral, negative (dismissive, conflict). Per walk: minutes of talk; share of positive and negative intervals.
|Validity and reliability|Second listener on 20% of walks; κ ≥ .60.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|As M-04.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-CL5; used-by → EM-02, EM-03; operationalizes → HV-06, HV-07.

= Measure - Safety while talking [M-06] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-06
|Name|Safety while talking
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke checklist. Grounded in HFC-02 (Lundin-Olsson et al., 1997).
|Construct measured|Whether talk on the walk raises fall risk or hides tiredness.
|Type|Quantitative (observational, with device events)
|Instrument or procedure|Listener-observer checklist per 2-minute interval, marked as talking or not talking; accelerometer events from the guide.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Stumbles; near-falls (balance lost, recovered without a fall); stops mid-sentence; tiredness signs (slows, leans on the rollator, breathless, asks to sit); falls; minutes past the set length. Per walk-hour, split by talking and not talking intervals.
|Validity and reliability|Bespoke. Observer and device counts compared; κ ≥ .60 between observers.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|As M-04.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F3-CL4; used-by → EM-02, EM-03; operationalizes → HV-04.

= Measure - The walkers' own view [M-07] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-07
|Name|The walkers' own view
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke. Self-report read alongside observation, never alone: residents rate their own experience higher than observers do (Custers et al., 2013).
|Construct measured|Whether the resident valued the walk, felt free to go or not, and wants to go again.
|Type|Mixed
|Instrument or procedure|(((
Resident, after each observed walk, only if willing and not distressed: three faces, "How was the walk?"; "Again?" (yes/no); "What was good about it?"

Listener-observer, before and at the start: pressure signs (agrees reluctantly; looks to partner or staff before answering; "if I must").
)))
|Scoring or coding scheme|Faces 1-3; "Again?" share of yes; "What was good" coded: company, place, talk, being out, nothing. Pressure signs per walk.
|Validity and reliability|Bespoke. Ceiling effects expected in self-report.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Low; one minute after the walk.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-CL7; used-by → EM-02, EM-03; operationalizes → HV-01, HV-07.

= Measure - Loneliness [M-08] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-08
|Name|Loneliness
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|De Jong Gierveld 6-item loneliness scale (De Jong Gierveld & Van Tilburg, 2006). Validated in older adults; not in dementia.
|Construct measured|Emotional and social loneliness.
|Type|Quantitative (self-report)
|Instrument or procedure|Six items read aloud by a researcher at the end of each phase (EM-03). Mild to moderate dementia only.
|Scoring or coding scheme|0-6, as in the source; emotional and social subscales reported separately.
|Validity and reliability|Validity in dementia uncertain; phases of 2-3 weeks are short for loneliness to change. Exploratory.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Five minutes per phase.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-CL9; used-by → EM-03; operationalizes → HV-06.

= Measure - Privacy, tone and trust codes [M-09] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-09
|Name|Privacy, tone and trust codes
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke code set from Niemeijer et al. (2010, 2015), Kitwood (1997), Williams et al. (2009) and the Mental Health Foundation (2016).
|Construct measured|Whether residents, partners, relatives and staff experience the System as watching, listening in, talking down, serving staff, revealing, or misleading.
|Type|Qualitative coding
|Instrument or procedure|Short interviews (EM-04).
|Scoring or coding scheme|W watched; L listened in on; T talked down to; D walked for others' sake ("taken out like a dog"); S secret revealed; U misled or fobbed off; N neutral or positive. A statement can have more than one code.
|Validity and reliability|Two coders on all interviews; κ ≥ .60.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Interviews; residents only if willing.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F2-CL2, UC02-F4-CL6, UC01-F1-CL7, UC01-F2-CL8; used-by → EM-04; operationalizes → HV-01 to HV-03, HV-08.

= Measure - Sensitive topics, errors and disclosure [M-10] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-10
|Name|Sensitive topics, errors and disclosure
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke count. Safeguard for VT-03 and VT-04.
|Construct measured|Whether painful questions reach the trusted person; how often the System gets a fact wrong, and whether it corrects itself; whether it reveals what it should not.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|(((
Script check: every prepared line and template, before use (EM-01).

Staged trials with known facts, including a wrong profile entry and a misheard topic (EM-01).

In use: session record (F6); listener-observer (S slips in M-04; System errors noticed by a walker, staff or the listener).
)))
|Scoring or coding scheme|(((
Deception by design: untruths, denials or playing along in the script (target 0).

Errors: wrong facts per 100 System statements; share corrected when contradicted; time to correct. Threshold: O-2.

Sensitive questions: deferred to the listed person (yes/no); minutes until they arrive.

Disclosure: items spoken without consent or against the map (target 0).
)))
|Validity and reliability|Staged error rates are exact. In use, only noticed errors are counted: an undercount. A disclosure, or an error that causes distress, stops the B phase for review (EM-03).
|Serves|Both: Verification (staged) and Validation (in use)
|Burden|None beyond EM-02.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC02-F4-PR4, UC02-F4-CL6, UC01-F1-PR1; used-by → EM-01, EM-02, EM-03; operationalizes → HV-03, HV-08.

= References =

//Cited from memory; checked in the evidence pass.//

* Custers, A. F. J. et al. (2013). Need fulfillment in the nursing home: resident and observer perspectives in relation to resident well-being. //European Journal of Ageing//, 10(3), 201-209.
* De Jong Gierveld, J. & Van Tilburg, T. (2006). A 6-item scale for overall, emotional, and social loneliness: confirmatory tests on survey data. //Research on Aging//, 28(5), 582-598.
* Dean, R., Proudfoot, R. & Lindesay, J. (1993). The quality of interactions schedule (QUIS): development, reliability and use in the evaluation of two domus units. //International Journal of Geriatric Psychiatry//, 8(10), 819-826.
* Forbes et al. (2015); Lundin-Olsson et al. (1997); Mental Health Foundation (2016); Orange et al. (1996); Schegloff et al. (1977). See a1.1 References.
* Kitwood (1997); Williams et al. (2009). See b3.1 References.
* Landis, J. R. & Koch, G. G. (1977). The measurement of observer agreement for categorical data. //Biometrics//, 33(1), 159-174.
* Lawton, M. P., Van Haitsma, K. & Klapper, J. (1996). Observed affect in nursing home residents with Alzheimer's disease. //Journal of Gerontology B//, 51(1), P3-P14.
* Lawton, M. P. et al. (1999). Observed affect and quality of life in dementia: further affirmations and problems. //Journal of Mental Health and Aging//, 5(1), 69-81.
* Mihailidis, A. et al. (2008). The COACH prompting system to assist older adults with dementia through handwashing: an efficacy study. //BMC Geriatrics//, 8, 28.
* Niemeijer, A. R. et al. (2010). Ethical and practical concerns of surveillance technologies in residential care for people with dementia or intellectual disabilities: an overview of the literature. //International Psychogeriatrics//. PMID 20199699.
* Niemeijer, A. R. et al. (2015). The experiences of people with dementia and intellectual disabilities with surveillance technologies in residential care. //Nursing Ethics//, 22(3), 307-320.
